WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.120
"Is Shrimp Good for You?
Antibiotic Resistance"

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.720
About 90% of shrimp

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.840
consumed in the United States
is imported,

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.880
and because much
imported seafood

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.200
is farm-raised
in confined conditions,

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.040
drugs are used to prevent
or treat disease

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.880
and increase survival rates,

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.640
and residues
of some of these drugs

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.720
can cause cancer
or allergic reactions

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.520
or contribute
to antibiotic resistance.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:27.520
Among different kinds
of seafood,

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.920
shrimp have had
the highest frequency

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.160
of veterinary drug violations,

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.520
primarily for nitrofurans
and chloramphenicol.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.880
The United States banned
chloramphenicol 35 years ago

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.680
due to a link with
a rare and often fatal disease

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.760
called aplastic anemia.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.400
Nitrofurans are also dangerous
because of their potential

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:47.760
cancer-causing properties.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.840
So, their use in animals
produced for human consumption

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.920
is similarly banned
in Europe and the United States.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.880
For something as potentially
dangerous as chloramphenicol,

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.880
governments around the world
have established

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.040
zero-tolerance policies,

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.240
which means that no residues
in food are permissible.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:06.000
However,
traces of chloramphenicol

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.000
have been detected in shrimp.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.600
Unlike most of the animals
we eat,

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.720
who have multiple organs
for drug excretion,

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.560
shrimp possess only one,

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.680
which may allow more antibiotics
to build up in them.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.200
But you don't know
until you put it to the test.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:22.560
There have been
four studies published

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.840
on the presence of banned drugs
in retail U.S. shrimp.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.080
One found that 92% of imported,
farm-raised shrimp

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.160
tested positive
for at least one drug

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:35.440
that is banned for use
in the United States.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:37.680
Another, of just
six shrimp from stores

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.200
in Arizona and California,

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.040
found drugs in two of them.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.880
The third tested dozens
purchased in Louisiana

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.880
from many
of our main shrimp importers,

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:48.800
and 70% tested positive

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.960
for the zero-tolerance
carcinogenic nitrofurantoin.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.080
Of all the countries,

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.000
Vietnam appeared
to be sending us shrimp

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.480
with the most types
of antibiotic residues.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.040
The 100% positives
in Bangladesh and Ecuador

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:02.960
were based on just
a single positive sample--

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.200
one out of one--
for both countries.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.800
The fourth, however,
found no antibiotic residues.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.280
Unfortunately,
only a small fraction of imports

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.840
are tested by the U.S.
Food and Drug Administration,

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:15.680
and, therefore,
the potential for shipments

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.280
with antibiotic tainted shrimp

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.080
to slip through the cracks
remains.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:21.520
And they're mostly from Asia,

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.240
which has been identified as
posing a very significant risk

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.600
to the global burden
of antibiotic resistance.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.320
Stocking densities on shrimp
farms are often very high,

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.160
which concentrates bacteria

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:33.920
and raises stress levels
in the animals,

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.640
which increases their
susceptibility to infection,

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.160
leading to outbreaks
that kill, like,

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.880
a billion shrimp at a time.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.280
Shrimp don't respond
to vaccination;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.200
so, to combat disease,

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.600
farmers employ
a range of chemicals,

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.240
the residues of which can end up
in the final product we may eat.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:53.840
Even in more
traditional systems,

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.040
where human and animal waste
is dumped into shrimp ponds,

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.120
the antibiotics
that end up in the manure

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.720
end up just increasing
overall antibiotic exposure.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.440
Aside from the potential for
fostering antibiotic resistance,

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.520
consuming antibiotic residues
can have a direct toxic effect,

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.760
and the constant traces
of antibiotics

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.080
can also disrupt
the human microbiome,

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.240
as has been demonstrated.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.160
The consumption
of shrimp containing

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:19.360
potentially unsafe levels

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.240
of antibiotics from aquaculture,
shrimp farming,

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.920
is a global concern
due to overuse of antibiotics.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.280
There is clearly an urgent need
for governments to regulate

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.560
the use of antibiotics
in crustacean production.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.920
In 2001,
chloramphenicol was detected

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.480
in shrimp imported
into Europe from Asia,

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:40.400
triggering a food scandal,

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.400
and the regulatory response
spilled over

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.560
to other major
seafood-importing countries,

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.160
including the United States.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:47.760
The U.S.
Food and Drug Administration

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.200
started testing and every year
has rejected shrimp

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.360
due to the presence
of antimicrobial drugs,

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:57.680
the most contaminated
of all tested seafood products.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.880
That shows the system
is working, right?

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.280
Well, only a small amount
of imported seafood

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:05.520
is actually inspected.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:06.800
How small?

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.068
For shrimp, about four samples
out of every 10 million pounds

00:04:12.068 --> 00:04:14.640
(4,535 tons)
of shrimp are tested.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.280
So, assuming there are about
25 shrimp in a pound (0.45 kg),

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:20.960
that's like one sample tested
for every 60 million shrimp.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.320
Of course,
they come in big batches,

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.920
yet the FDA only tests
about 0.1%

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:30.000
of the million annual shipments
that come in every year.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.760
Based on this level of testing,

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:35.280
seafood shipments from
a foreign processing facility

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.400
would have a roughly
one-in-a-thousand chance

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.240
of being selected by the FDA
for drug residue testing.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:46.000
Is that because positive
findings are so rare? No.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.440
About every one in every 10
shipments of shrimp tested

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:53.120
turns up contaminated
with unsafe drug residues.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:55.760
So, that means
like a hundred shipments

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:58.960
of the other 999
that weren't tested

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:00.880
may also be contaminated;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:05.200
yet, they can go straight to
restaurants or grocery stores.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.280
And we only test
for a few drugs.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:10.560
Europe, Japan, and Canada
are picking up

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:12.480
seven to 10 different drugs,

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:14.480
whereas we only look for two.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.120
So, we are likely missing
the wider range of drugs

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.040
picked up by other countries.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:21.040
Why doesn't the FDA test more?

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:22.640
According to FDA officials,

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:24.960
they don't have the resources
to test more.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.400
Okay, said the GAO,

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.240
which is
the congressional watchdog,

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:31.280
how about forcing the countries
that are sending the shrimp

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:34.160
to test for drugs
before they ship them here?

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:35.600
Let them pay for it.

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.280
That's what Europe does.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:40.720
Well, the FDA decided
not to pursue such agreements

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:41.520
with other countries

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:44.720
to require them to test seafood
exported to the United States

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:48.160
for unsafe drug residues,
as Europe does.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:49.680
Why not?

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:52.960
They didn't think some of the
countries that send us shrimp

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:54.160
might be able to,

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:56.800
because they lack
the laboratory infrastructure

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:58.240
and capabilities needed

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:01.760
to test for the drugs
of concern to the FDA.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:04.800
I'm reminded of an outbreak
investigation of salmonella

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:05.520
linked to shrimp,

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:08.160
where the investigators had
to explain to the exporters

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:09.920
that they probably shouldn't be

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:13.040
scooping up shrimp
off the floor.

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:16.560
But wait, countries exporting
to Europe are able to comply;

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:19.920
so, what ends up happening
is that the United States

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.640
may still be a market
for at least some seafood

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:25.680
that did not meet
the safety standards of Europe.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:29.920
As a result, shrimp that cannot
be shipped to European countries

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:32.800
because of concerns
about violative drug residues

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:36.640
may be shipped to
the United States, no problem.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:39.120
It's like the use
of some coloring agents

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:41.840
used to disguise
shrimp diseases:

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:44.800
approved in the United States
but prohibited elsewhere

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:49.040
due to concerns about causing
ADHD symptoms in children.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:52.240
Countries know they can
always send products here,

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:55.280
and even if they do use drugs
they aren't supposed to,

00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:00.000
there's only a one-in-a-thousand
chance we'll even check.

